      "urpx:forRatePlan": {
        "@id": "gpc:iop"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "gpc:cc-iop-2026-06-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-iop-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 9 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Hours-use bands. The sheet prices kWh in bands bounded by hours times the Billing Demand: not greater than 200 hours (in blocks of kWh) and over 200 hours. A urpx:ServiceBracket bound is a fixed number (https://github.com/urpx-org/urpx/issues/87), so each band's kWh is a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod, and the first band carries the kWh blocks; a consumer has to split the month's kWh by the Billing Demand. clj-urpx sums every energy ledger for a marginal rate, and the rider subtotals sum the same prices, so this filing's recorded marginal rate adds each higher band's price to the first block's, where the sheet prices a month's first kWh at the first block's price alone. (2) Billing Demand. Billing Demand is a calculated MetricInput on the 30-minute kW demand, with the sheet's ratchets over the current month and the preceding eleven, its floors and its rule for a customer changing from a time of use tariff stated in its description and formula. URPX v0.5.1 does not evaluate a calculationFormula, so a consumer has to compute it from twelve months of demand history, contract capacity and contract minimum. The sheet's seasons for it are billing months. (3) Fuel seasons by billing month. FCR-27 prices June through September and October through May, which Schedule R's bills show is read by the billing month a bill is rendered in. The JSON-LD gates the fuel PriceDefinitions on calendar months, so usage in the weeks a billing period runs ahead of its billing month takes the other season's fuel price. (4) Voltage level and franchise fee location. FCR-27's three voltage levels and MFF-11's two locations are crossed in one ProfileAlternatives set of six alternatives, because URPX v0.5.1 allows one set per PlanElements and the franchise fee's energy subtotal must include that voltage level's fuel prices. A consumer selects one alternative, for example Secondary Distribution inside city limits. (5) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 and DSM-C-15 amounts. Its multiplier Prices here are based on the energy-and-fuel subtotal and on the non-energy charges' Prices, not on the ECCR and DSM-C multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on those two amounts, about 0.44 percent of the base bill inside city limits, is not carried. (6) Excess Reactive Demand. Where a power factor below 95 percent lagging is indicated, the Company may install metering for reactive demand and bills kVAR above one-third of the measured kW at $0.43 per kVAR. The JSON-LD carries the $0.43 on a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod; a consumer has to compute the excess kVAR, and supplies none for a customer the Company does not meter for it. (7) On-Peak surcharge. Consumption in the On-Peak period, every hour that is not Off-Peak (so noon to 8:00 p.m. on weekdays in the billing months June through September, except the observed Memorial Day, Independence Day and Labor Day), causes a surcharge of 25 percent of the total amount calculated on the tariff, including all adjustments except Fuel Cost Recovery, the Municipal Franchise Fee and taxes. A surcharge on the whole bill triggered by any On-Peak consumption is not a price per unit in a period, and no Price carries it; a modeled bill for a customer who uses energy On-Peak is low by a quarter of its non-fuel charges. (8) Minimum Monthly Bill. The sheet sets it at the $43.72 Basic Service Charge plus $12.66 per kW of actual On-Peak demand in excess of 30 kW, plus excess kVAR charges, ECCR, DSM, Fuel Cost Recovery on the current month's kWh and the Municipal Franchise Fee. A minimum is a floor under the bill, which no Price expresses, so it is not carried; a modeled bill below it is low by the difference. (9) Term of Contract. Five years. Nothing is priced by it and it is not carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
